    "result_text": "The build is green. \n\nI implemented the city-capacity rule following the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions:\n\n1. **`EnsureCityCapacity`** function \u2014 counts existing owners in the request's city (case-insensitively, matching `AssignCustomerCode`'s per-city logic) and throws `CityAtCapacityException` when the count reaches 50. It runs before the owner is saved, so the count correctly excludes the owner being created.\n\n2. **`CityAtCapacityException`** \u2014 a checked exception (`extends Exception`) carrying the city and current count, mirroring `DuplicateHouseholdException`/`DuplicateTelephoneException`.\n\n3. **`CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler`** \u2014 responds 409 (`HttpStatus.CONFLICT`) with a `ProblemDetail`, same shape as the other conflict handlers.\n\n4. **Escalation YAML** (`officefloor/escalation/...CityAtCapacityException.yml`) wiring the exception to its handler.\n\n5. **Pipeline wiring** in `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted the `ensureCityCapacity` step (under `transaction` governance) between `ensureUniqueHousehold` and `build`.\n\nThe provided acceptance test (`coreRejectsWhenCityAtCapacity`) and the full suite pass.",
