    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is a faithful, additive copy of the patterns already established in this package:\n\n- **`EnsureCityCapacity`** sits alongside `EnsureUniqueOwnerTelephone` and `EnsureUniqueOwnerHousehold` as a pre-`build` guard step, uses the same `@Val OwnerFieldsDto request` + `OwnerRepository` signature, and throws a checked exception \u2014 exactly the sibling idiom. Its `findAll()`-loop with `equalsIgnoreCase` counting mirrors `AssignCustomerCode` and `AssignNamesakeCount` (the per-city / per-namesake counting convention this codebase already uses), so it does not introduce a novel or misplaced idiom. The magic number is named (`CITY_CAPACITY`) and the javadoc explains the pre-save timing, matching the neighbors.\n- **`CityAtCapacityException`** is a structural twin of `DuplicateHouseholdException` / `DuplicateTelephoneException` (checked, message built in constructor, typed getters, javadoc pointing to its handler).\n- **`CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler`** matches `DuplicateHouseholdExceptionHandler` / `DuplicateTelephoneExceptionHandler` line-for-line, reusing the shared `ProblemDetails.build(...)` helper and the `field` / `rejectedValue` property convention (409, field `city`).\n- The escalation YAML and the POST pipeline wiring (`govern: [ transaction ]`, inserted before `build`) follow the AGENTS.md conventions and existing file.\n\nLogic is in the right unit (one concern per class), nothing is duplicated beyond the per-city counting loop that the codebase has already standardized on, and no class is becoming a catch-all. A maintainer here would find it entirely natural.",
